# What is translated of this program's messages, in Russian.
#
# Keyed by the English wording, the same as the German file beside it.
# Part of the program's texts stand here -- the captions the window
# itself carries, not the report that runs beside them. Every text that
# is not in this file appears in English: T() gives back the key it was
# handed when it finds no entry, so what is missing reads rather than
# breaks.
#
# All of the program's texts stand here since 8.9.2026 -- the captions
# the window carries and the report that runs beside them, Resolve
# included. Until then it was the captions alone, 335 of 1451.
#
# The 1130 added on 8.9.2026 were written with the traps of the Spanish
# run written down in front of them (docs/notes/sprachen_fallen.md) and
# read back as a sample afterwards: 2.7 to 5.0 faults per hundred
# entries checked, against 8.8 in Spanish, which had no such list. That
# says how the file was made; it does not say a Russian speaker has
# seen it, because none has.
#
# These translations were written by Claude, an Anthropic model, out of
# what it knows. No translation service was asked, no Russian speaker
# has read them, and nobody has reviewed a single line. Treat every
# entry as a first draft. Correcting one costs nothing but this file:
# delete an entry and that text is English again.
#
# One entry per message: msgid is the English wording the
# program is written in, msgstr what is said instead. An
# entry left out appears in English.
#
# A counted thing is the exception, and Russian is why the machinery
# for it exists: English picks between one and everything else, Russian
# between three. Such an entry carries msgid_plural and msgstr[0],
# msgstr[1], msgstr[2] in place of the single msgstr, and the header
# line below says which count wants which of them -- [0] where the
# count ends in 1 but not in 11, [1] where it ends in 2, 3 or 4 but not
# in 12 to 14, [2] everywhere else. The wordings are read as a list, so
# a form left empty costs that form and not the entry.
#
# The English plural keeps its own ordinary entry beside such a group,
# and it is not spare: it is what a run falls back on where the rule
# cannot be read at all, and Russian then says the many-wording instead
# of English. The English singular has no entry of its own -- that
# would be the same msgid twice -- so in that one case a count of one
# comes out English.
